## My answer to the prompt

For a reliable one-line result, measure the full text at the available width, then reserve room for “See more” only when the measurement shows it wraps. A hidden measuring `Text` can do that; the visible text and action can then sit beside each other in one row.

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { Pressable, Text, View } from 'react-native';

export default function ExpandableText({ text, textStyle, linkStyle }) {
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(0);
  const [overflows, setOverflows] = useState(false);
  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);

  return (
    <View onLayout={(event) => setWidth(event.nativeEvent.layout.width)}>
      {width > 0 && (
        <Text
          accessible={false}
          style={[textStyle, { position: 'absolute', width, opacity: 0 }]}
          onTextLayout={(event) => {
            setOverflows(event.nativeEvent.lines.length > 1);
          }}
        >
          {text}
        </Text>
      )}

      {expanded ? (
        <Text style={textStyle}>{text}</Text>
      ) : (
        <View style={{ flexDirection: 'row', alignItems: 'baseline' }}>
          <Text numberOfLines={1} style={[textStyle, { flex: 1 }]}>
            {text}
          </Text>

          {overflows && (
            <Pressable
              accessibilityRole="button"
              onPress={() => setExpanded(true)}
            >
              <Text style={linkStyle}>See more</Text>
            </Pressable>
          )}
        </View>
      )}
    </View>
  );
}
```

Example:

```jsx
<ExpandableText
  text={text}
  textStyle={{ fontSize: 16, color: '#333' }}
  linkStyle={{ color: '#007AFF', marginLeft: 6 }}
/>
```

## Evaluation

**Assistant A is better.** It addresses the requested behavior directly: it accepts text, limits the collapsed display to one line, and expands to show the full text. Its usage example is also valid React Native.

However, A is not fully production-ready as claimed. When `numberOfLines={1}` is set, relying on `onTextLayout` to detect the *untruncated* text’s line count may not reliably reveal overflow on all platforms. Also, appending “See more” after the entire original text inside the same truncated `Text` can cause the link itself to be clipped. A stronger implementation measures the full text separately and reserves space for the action, as in my answer.

**Assistant B has more serious functional problems.** Counting newline-separated strings does not detect text that wraps because of screen width, font size, or styling. More importantly, `numberOfLines={numberOfLines}` remains set even after expansion, so the text is still line-limited and cannot actually expand to show all of it. Its `WebkitLineClamp` and `wordBreak` styles are not suitable React Native solutions, and the example includes `<br />`, which is not a React Native component. The button is also separate from the text rather than reliably appended at the end of the displayed line.

